export const DATA_76: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-111", num: 111, title: "Đề 111", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Earth Hour: Switching Off for the Planet", paragraphs: ["Every year, on the last Saturday of March, millions of people around the world participate in one of the most recognisable environmental events on the global calendar: Earth Hour. For sixty minutes, individuals, families, businesses, and governments turn off their non-essential lights as a symbolic gesture of solidarity with the planet and a visible statement of concern about climate change. From the illuminated skylines of New York, Paris, and Sydney to small towns in developing nations, the collective dimming of lights creates a powerful and memorable image that has made Earth Hour one of the most widely recognised environmental campaigns in history.", "Earth Hour was first held in Sydney, Australia, on 31 March 2007, when the World Wildlife Fund (WWF) Australia partnered with the city's residents and businesses to switch off lights for one hour as a demonstration of concern about global warming. The response exceeded all expectations: approximately 2.2 million homes and businesses in Sydney participated, and the event attracted immediate international attention. The following year, 2008, Earth Hour went global, with cities in more than thirty-five countries joining the campaign. By 2023, the event had grown to include participants in more than 190 countries and territories, making it one of the largest voluntary environmental actions ever organised.", "The mechanics of Earth Hour are deliberately simple. Participants are asked to switch off lights and non-essential electrical appliances for exactly one hour at 8:30 pm local time on the designated Saturday. Iconic landmarks around the world — including the Sydney Opera House, the Eiffel Tower, the Empire State Building, and the Acropolis in Athens — go dark for the hour, creating striking visual moments that are widely shared on social media and broadcast by news organisations worldwide. These images, arguably more than the energy savings themselves, constitute the primary mechanism through which Earth Hour communicates its message: that collective human action, however symbolic, can be visible, coordinated, and globally resonant.", "Critics of Earth Hour have raised questions about the actual environmental impact of the event. The electricity saved during the single hour of participation is, by any objective measure, negligible when set against annual global energy consumption. Some environmental scientists have pointed out that the surge in electricity demand that sometimes follows the end of Earth Hour — as participants switch their appliances back on simultaneously — can briefly increase carbon emissions rather than reduce them. Others have noted that the event can create a false sense of accomplishment, encouraging participants to feel that a single symbolic gesture is sufficient engagement with a problem that requires sustained systemic change.", "Supporters of Earth Hour respond that these criticisms, while technically valid, miss the point of the event. Earth Hour, they argue, was never primarily an energy-saving exercise but a global communications campaign designed to raise awareness, build solidarity, and demonstrate to governments and corporations that public concern about climate change is real and widespread. The organisers at WWF have consistently framed the event as a starting point rather than an end in itself, encouraging participants to extend their commitment beyond the single hour through pledges, volunteer activities, and advocacy for policy change. Research conducted after the event has suggested that participation in Earth Hour is associated with increased environmental awareness and a greater likelihood of adopting pro-environmental behaviours in the months that follow.", "Beyond awareness-raising, Earth Hour has also served as a platform for fundraising and conservation project funding. In numerous countries, the campaign has been used to generate financial support for local environmental initiatives, from coral reef restoration in the Philippines to wildfire prevention in South Africa. Schools and universities around the world incorporate Earth Hour into environmental education programmes, using the event as an entry point for broader discussions about energy use, climate science, and individual and collective responsibility. Community events — candlelit dinners, outdoor concerts, and public astronomy sessions — have been organised around the hour of darkness, transforming what could be a private and passive act of switching off a light into a shared social experience.", "Whether Earth Hour is ultimately more symbol than substance remains a matter of ongoing debate among environmentalists, scientists, and policymakers. What seems difficult to deny, however, is that the event has successfully accomplished something genuinely rare in the crowded and contentious landscape of environmental advocacy: it has created an annual moment of global attention that is simultaneously accessible to participants of every income level, educational background, and nationality, requiring nothing more than the willingness to reach for a light switch. In a world where the scale and complexity of the climate crisis can easily provoke despair or disengagement, that accessibility may be one of Earth Hour's most underappreciated contributions."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Earth Hour was originally a local Sydney event that grew unexpectedly into a global campaign, and its future success depends on whether it can continue attracting participation from new countries each year.", "Earth Hour is an ineffective environmental campaign because the electricity it saves is negligible and its symbolic gestures distract the public from the urgent need for systemic policy change.", "The primary contribution of Earth Hour has been its fundraising success, which has generated hundreds of millions of dollars for conservation projects in developing countries around the world.", "Earth Hour is a globally recognised annual event that, despite legitimate questions about its direct environmental impact, has demonstrated significant value as a tool for raising awareness, building solidarity, and inspiring broader engagement."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what happened when Earth Hour was first held in Sydney in 2007?", options: ["The event was considered a disappointment because fewer than one million residents participated, prompting WWF Australia to redesign the campaign before attempting to take it global in 2008.", "The event caused a temporary blackout across parts of Sydney because so many residents turned off their power at the same moment, overwhelming the electrical grid.", "Approximately 2.2 million homes and businesses participated, the response exceeded expectations, and the event attracted immediate international attention.", "The Sydney city government mandated participation by switching off all public lighting, and private citizens were required by law to turn off their lights for the duration of the hour."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, at what time are participants around the world asked to turn off their lights during Earth Hour?", options: ["At 9:00 pm Greenwich Mean Time, so that participants in all countries turn off their lights at the same absolute moment regardless of their local time zone.", "At sunset local time, so that the transition from natural to artificial light provides a consistent visual reference point for participants in different geographic locations.", "At 8:30 pm local time on the designated Saturday, allowing participants in every time zone to join the event at the same point in their own evening.", "At midnight local time on the last Saturday of March, when the contrast between lit and darkened buildings is most visually dramatic for aerial and satellite photography."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is mentioned as an example of a conservation project supported through Earth Hour fundraising?", options: ["Reforestation of the Amazon rainforest in Brazil, funded through donations collected from participants in more than forty countries during the 2022 campaign.", "Construction of solar-powered community centres in rural India, financed through a joint Earth Hour partnership with the United Nations Environment Programme.", "Coral reef restoration in the Philippines and wildfire prevention programmes in South Africa, funded through Earth Hour campaign activities in those countries.", "Protection of endangered snow leopard habitats in Nepal and Tibet, supported by pledges made by Earth Hour participants at events held at partner universities."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"These\" at the beginning of the second sentence in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the news organisations and television broadcasters that cover Earth Hour and transmit images of darkened skylines to audiences around the world", "the iconic landmarks around the world that go dark during Earth Hour, including the Eiffel Tower, the Sydney Opera House, and the Empire State Building", "the non-essential electrical appliances that participants are asked to switch off for exactly one hour at the designated time each year during Earth Hour", "the striking visual moments created when famous landmarks go dark, which are widely shared on social media and broadcast by news organisations"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"negligible\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["so small as to be unimportant or not worth taking into consideration when evaluating the overall impact of the campaign on global energy consumption", "difficult to calculate accurately because the number of participants varies so widely from country to country and from year to year across different regions", "controversial and disputed among energy scientists who disagree about the most reliable methodology for measuring short-term reductions in electricity use", "surprising and counterintuitive, given the enormous number of participants who turn off their lights simultaneously during the sixty-minute event each year"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"solidarity\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a sense of unity and shared commitment among people who stand together in support of a common cause or in response to a shared concern", "legal obligation imposed by international agreements on participating nations to reduce their carbon emissions by a specified percentage each decade", "public recognition awarded to individuals or communities that have made exceptional contributions to environmental protection over an extended period", "financial contribution made by individuals and organisations to support a cause they believe is important but are not directly involved in managing"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following criticisms of Earth Hour is NOT mentioned in the passage?", options: ["The event may create a false sense of accomplishment that makes participants feel a symbolic gesture is sufficient engagement with the climate crisis", "The electricity saved during the event is negligible compared with annual global energy consumption", "The event receives disproportionate media coverage relative to its actual environmental contribution, which crowds out reporting on more effective climate initiatives", "The surge in demand that follows the hour can briefly increase carbon emissions rather than reduce them"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 5 about how Earth Hour's organisers measure the event's success?", options: ["They measure success primarily by the total number of kilowatt-hours of electricity saved globally during the sixty-minute event, which they report annually in official campaign statistics.", "They regard success as having been achieved if the event generates enough revenue through donations to fund at least one major international conservation project per year.", "They consider the event successful only if it attracts participation from a larger number of countries and territories than the previous year, indicating continued growth in global reach.", "They evaluate success based on the level of public awareness raised, the solidarity demonstrated, and whether participants are inspired to extend their environmental commitment beyond the single hour."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward Earth Hour as expressed in the final paragraph?", options: ["Completely neutral, presenting the competing arguments about Earth Hour's value without offering any indication of which position the author finds more persuasive.", "Sharply critical, concluding that Earth Hour is fundamentally a marketing exercise that exploits public concern about climate change without producing any meaningful environmental benefit.", "Cautiously positive, acknowledging the debate about its substance while recognising that its accessibility and global reach represent a genuine and underappreciated contribution to environmental advocacy.", "Unreservedly enthusiastic, arguing that Earth Hour is the single most effective environmental campaign ever devised and that critics of the event fundamentally misunderstand its purpose."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Daily Habits and the Architecture of a Healthy Life", paragraphs: ["In the study of human behaviour and wellbeing, few concepts have attracted as sustained and as practically consequential a body of research as the habit. Defined broadly as a behaviour that has become automatic through repetition — performed with minimal conscious deliberation in response to contextual cues — the habit is now understood by psychologists and neuroscientists to account for a substantial proportion of daily human activity. Estimates suggest that between thirty and fifty percent of the actions people perform each day are governed by habit rather than by conscious choice, meaning that the cumulative shape of a human life is determined less by deliberate decision-making than by the entrenched patterns of routine that individuals establish, often without fully intending to, over months and years of repeated behaviour.", "The neuroscientific basis of habit formation is well established. When a behaviour is repeated consistently in a given context, the neural pathways associated with that behaviour become progressively more efficient through a process known as long-term potentiation — the strengthening of synaptic connections that occurs when neurons fire repeatedly in coordinated patterns. Over time, the behaviour becomes encoded in the basal ganglia, a region of the brain associated with procedural learning and automatic motor control, rather than in the prefrontal cortex, which governs deliberate, effortful decision-making. This neurological shift explains why deeply ingrained habits are so difficult to break: the behaviour has been literally restructured at the level of brain circuitry, and dismantling it requires sustained effort to override automatic processes that the brain has optimised for efficiency.", "The architecture of a habit, as described in influential research by psychologist Ann Graybiel and later popularised by journalist Charles Duhigg, consists of three components: a cue, a routine, and a reward. The cue is the contextual trigger — a time of day, a location, an emotional state, or the presence of another person — that initiates the habitual behaviour. The routine is the behaviour itself. The reward is the positive outcome — physiological, emotional, or social — that reinforces the neural pathway and increases the likelihood that the behaviour will be repeated when the cue recurs. Understanding this structure is particularly important for anyone seeking to change an existing habit or establish a new one, since research suggests that the most effective strategies for habit modification involve targeting the cue and reward rather than attempting to suppress the routine through willpower alone.", "Sleep is among the daily habits most extensively documented as a determinant of physical and mental health. Adults who consistently obtain between seven and nine hours of sleep per night show significantly lower rates of cardiovascular disease, obesity, type 2 diabetes, and depression compared with those who regularly sleep fewer than six hours. During sleep, the brain consolidates memories, clears metabolic waste products through the glymphatic system, and regulates the hormones that control appetite, stress response, and immune function. Despite this evidence, surveys across industrialised nations consistently find that large proportions of adults report chronically insufficient sleep, driven by work demands, screen use, and the cultural valorisation of productivity over rest. The consequences of this collective sleep deprivation — reduced cognitive performance, impaired emotional regulation, and elevated disease risk — represent one of the most significant and most neglected public health challenges of the modern era.", "Physical activity habits exhibit a similarly powerful relationship with long-term health outcomes. The World Health Organization recommends that adults engage in at least one hundred and fifty minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic activity per week, yet global surveys indicate that a substantial minority of adults in both high- and low-income countries fail to meet this threshold. Regular physical activity reduces the risk of cardiovascular disease, certain cancers, and metabolic disorders; it improves mood, cognitive function, and sleep quality; and it is associated with lower rates of anxiety and depression. Critically, the health benefits of exercise are not dependent on intensive training regimens: research has consistently found that even modest increases in daily movement — taking the stairs, walking to nearby destinations, or incorporating short activity breaks into sedentary work routines — produce measurable improvements in health outcomes when sustained over time.", "Nutritional habits represent a third domain in which the cumulative effects of daily choices have profound long-term consequences. The relationship between diet and chronic disease is now among the most extensively studied areas of epidemiology, with robust evidence linking diets high in ultra-processed foods, added sugars, and saturated fats to elevated risks of heart disease, stroke, type 2 diabetes, and certain cancers. Conversely, dietary patterns characterised by high consumption of vegetables, fruits, whole grains, legumes, and unsaturated fats — broadly consistent with what researchers describe as a Mediterranean-style diet — are associated with significantly lower rates of these conditions and with greater longevity. These findings have important implications not only for individual behaviour but also for food policy, urban planning, and the design of food environments that either facilitate or obstruct healthy eating for different socioeconomic groups.", "The challenge of establishing and maintaining healthy daily habits is complicated by the fact that behaviour does not occur in a social or environmental vacuum. The built environment, social norms, economic resources, and cultural practices of the communities in which people live all exert powerful influences on the habits they form. Access to safe walking and cycling infrastructure, proximity to parks and recreational facilities, the availability and affordability of fresh food, and the social modelling provided by family members and peers all shape the daily behaviour patterns of individuals in ways that individual willpower and personal motivation alone cannot overcome. Recognising this reality does not diminish the importance of individual agency but it does suggest that durable improvements in population-level health habits require changes not only in individual behaviour but in the social and physical environments that make certain habits easier or harder to form and maintain."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Sleep is the single most important daily habit for long-term health, and all other lifestyle improvements are significantly less effective unless individuals first achieve consistently adequate rest each night.", "Research on habit formation has demonstrated that the three-component cue-routine-reward model is the only scientifically valid framework for understanding human behaviour and designing effective behaviour-change interventions.", "Daily habits are neurologically encoded behavioural patterns that profoundly shape long-term health outcomes across multiple domains, and their formation and change are influenced by both individual and environmental factors.", "The primary obstacle to healthier daily habits is the lack of individual motivation and willpower, and education campaigns that provide people with accurate health information are sufficient to produce lasting behavioural change."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are deeply ingrained habits so difficult to break?", options: ["The rewards associated with habitual behaviours are so physiologically powerful that the discomfort of changing a habit always outweighs the perceived benefits of the alternative behaviour.", "They are reinforced continuously by social pressure from family members and peers who share the same habits and who actively discourage any attempt at behavioural change.", "The behaviour has been restructured at the level of brain circuitry in the basal ganglia, meaning that overriding it requires sustained effort to counteract processes the brain has optimised for efficiency.", "They are stored in the prefrontal cortex, where deliberate decision-making occurs, making them highly resistant to the kind of automatic override that the basal ganglia is capable of performing."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes the most effective strategy for changing an existing habit?", options: ["Replacing the existing reward with a more intensely pleasurable alternative, so that the new behaviour becomes associated with a stronger positive reinforcement than the old habit provided.", "Focusing primarily on suppressing the habitual routine through sheer willpower and self-discipline, which researchers have found to be the most reliable method of overcoming automatic behaviour.", "Targeting the cue and the reward rather than attempting to suppress the routine by willpower alone, since research suggests this approach is more effective for habit modification.", "Eliminating all contextual cues associated with the habit from one's environment, so that the brain never receives the trigger that initiates the automatic behavioural sequence."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following occurs in the brain during sleep?", options: ["The brain increases production of cortisol and adrenaline during the deepest stages of sleep, preparing the body for the physical and cognitive demands of the following day.", "The brain consolidates memories, removes metabolic waste through the glymphatic system, and regulates hormones that control appetite, stress response, and immune function.", "The basal ganglia temporarily suspends all habitual behaviour programmes during sleep, allowing the brain to reset and reprogram daily routines for the following day.", "The prefrontal cortex enters a heightened state of activity during deep sleep, during which it processes and resolves unresolved emotional conflicts from the preceding day."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"entrenched\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["externally imposed by social convention or institutional expectation, rather than arising organically from an individual's own preferences and personal experiences", "consciously chosen and deliberately maintained by the individual, reflecting a considered commitment to a particular way of organising one's daily activities and routines", "recently acquired and still fragile, not yet sufficiently reinforced by repetition to have become fully automatic or resistant to disruption by new competing behaviours", "deeply established and firmly fixed through long repetition, making them very difficult to alter or remove even when a person consciously wishes to change them"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"valorisation\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the scientific validation of a health intervention through randomised controlled trials, confirming that it produces measurable improvements in the outcomes it is designed to address", "the financial measurement of the economic costs associated with reduced workplace productivity caused by chronic sleep deprivation among employed adults", "the process of assigning high social worth or prestige to something — in this context, the cultural elevation of productivity and busyness as markers of admirable personal character", "the formal recognition awarded by professional organisations to individuals who demonstrate outstanding commitment to occupational performance and efficiency over an extended career"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"These\" at the beginning of the final sentence of paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["the research findings linking dietary patterns to chronic disease risk and longevity, which have implications for individual behaviour, food policy, and the design of food environments", "the Mediterranean-style dietary patterns characterised by high consumption of vegetables, fruits, whole grains, legumes, and unsaturated fats described earlier in the paragraph", "the socioeconomic groups who face structural obstacles to healthy eating due to the design of food environments, limited economic resources, and constrained access to fresh produce", "the chronic diseases — including heart disease, stroke, type 2 diabetes, and certain cancers — associated with diets high in ultra-processed foods and added sugars"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit of regular physical activity?", options: ["Improved mood, cognitive function, and quality of sleep", "Lower rates of anxiety and depression among adults who meet the recommended activity thresholds", "Increased bone density and reduced risk of osteoporosis in adults over the age of fifty", "Reduced risk of cardiovascular disease, certain cancers, and metabolic disorders"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 2 about the relationship between habit and conscious decision-making?", options: ["Conscious decision-making and habitual behaviour are entirely separate neurological processes that never interact, meaning that changes in deliberate behaviour have no effect on the formation of new automatic habits.", "Once a habit has been formed, conscious decision-making becomes permanently incapable of influencing the behaviour, meaning that habitual actions can never be altered by deliberate intention.", "The development of strong habits in the basal ganglia enhances the capacity of the prefrontal cortex to make more efficient decisions in other areas of life by reducing the cognitive load of routine tasks.", "Because habitual behaviours are processed in a different brain region from conscious decisions, changing deeply ingrained habits requires more than willpower — it demands strategies that work at the level of automatic processing."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's primary purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To provide a step-by-step practical guide for individuals who want to build healthier daily routines by applying the cue-routine-reward framework to their personal sleep, exercise, and dietary habits.", "To demonstrate that the neuroscientific study of habit formation has conclusively resolved all major questions in the fields of public health and behavioural medicine, making further research unnecessary.", "To present a research-grounded account of how habits are formed and why they matter for health, while emphasising that sustainable change requires attention to both individual behaviour and the broader social environment.", "To argue that governments bear sole responsibility for improving population health habits and that individual behaviour change campaigns are an ineffective substitute for structural policy reform."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Technology in Education: Promise, Peril, and Pedagogy", paragraphs: ["Few developments in modern education have generated as much enthusiasm — and as much scepticism — as the integration of digital technology into teaching and learning. From interactive whiteboards and tablet devices in primary classrooms to massive open online courses and artificial intelligence-driven tutoring platforms at university level, the technological transformation of education has proceeded with remarkable speed over the past two decades. Proponents argue that technology can personalise learning at a scale that traditional classroom instruction cannot achieve, democratise access to high-quality educational resources across geographic and socioeconomic divides, and equip students with the digital competencies essential for participation in a knowledge-based global economy. Critics, however, contend that the adoption of educational technology has frequently outpaced the evidence base for its effectiveness, and that the enthusiasm of technology companies, policymakers, and administrators has not always been shared by the teachers and learners who bear the consequences of implementation decisions.", "The concept of personalised learning — the idea that technology can adapt the pace, content, and mode of instruction to the individual needs and learning profiles of each student — represents perhaps the most compelling and most contested claim made on behalf of educational technology. Adaptive learning platforms, which use algorithms to analyse student performance data and adjust the difficulty and type of tasks presented accordingly, have been deployed in schools and universities across the world with considerable investment and enthusiasm. [1] Some evaluations have reported positive outcomes, particularly for students who have fallen behind in core subjects such as mathematics and reading, where the ability to provide immediate, individualised feedback can help learners identify and address specific gaps in understanding. However, the research evidence is more mixed than advocates typically acknowledge: a substantial number of rigorous studies have found effect sizes that are modest at best, and concerns have been raised about whether algorithmic systems can adequately capture the social, emotional, and motivational dimensions of learning that skilled human teachers address through relational and contextual judgment.", "The question of access and equity occupies a central position in debates about educational technology, and it admits of no straightforward resolution. On one hand, digital platforms and open educational resources have the potential to extend access to high-quality learning materials to students in remote or underserved communities who lack access to well-resourced schools, specialist teachers, or physical libraries. Massive open online courses (MOOCs), which at their peak enrolled millions of learners from countries that would otherwise have had no access to instruction from leading universities, represent a dramatic illustration of this potential. [2] On the other hand, research has repeatedly demonstrated that the benefits of educational technology are not distributed equitably: students from higher-income households, who are more likely to have reliable internet access, suitable devices, and adult support at home, consistently derive greater benefit from digital learning opportunities than their lower-income peers. Rather than closing the educational achievement gap, technology can widen it when the infrastructural and social conditions necessary for effective use are unevenly distributed.", "Teacher professional development represents a critical and frequently underestimated variable in the equation of educational technology effectiveness. The introduction of new technological tools into classrooms without adequate investment in the training, coaching, and ongoing support of the teachers who use them is a pattern that has been documented repeatedly in educational technology research, and it is consistently associated with suboptimal outcomes. Technology does not teach; teachers do. The effectiveness of a digital tool depends not on the sophistication of its design but on the pedagogical skill with which it is integrated into a coherent instructional sequence, matched to the learning goals of a specific lesson, and deployed in a way that enhances rather than displaces the relational and motivational work that skilled teachers do. [3] Systems that invest in technology without investing equivalently in teacher capacity are, in effect, purchasing hardware and software while neglecting the human infrastructure upon which their educational value ultimately depends.", "The impact of screen time and digital device use on students' cognitive development and psychological wellbeing has emerged as one of the most contentious areas of educational technology research. Studies examining the effects of prolonged screen exposure on attention span, reading comprehension, and the capacity for sustained concentration have produced a complex and sometimes contradictory body of evidence. Some researchers have argued that the cognitive demands of navigating digital environments — multitasking, rapid information processing, and frequent context-switching — may cultivate certain forms of distributed attention at the expense of the deep, focused reading associated with the acquisition of complex conceptual knowledge. [4] At the same time, digital tools specifically designed to support reading — electronic texts with embedded vocabulary support, interactive annotations, and comprehension monitoring features — have shown promise for students with reading difficulties, suggesting that the relationship between technology and literacy is highly dependent on how digital tools are designed and how they are used.", "The pedagogical debate about educational technology ultimately reduces to a question that technology itself cannot answer: what is education for? If the primary purpose of schooling is to transmit a defined body of knowledge and skills efficiently and at scale, then well-designed technological systems may indeed offer significant advantages over traditional instruction. If, however, education is understood as a fundamentally relational and developmental process — one in which the intellectual and moral formation of young people depends on sustained engagement with knowledgeable, caring adults and with the irreducibly social experience of learning alongside peers — then technology's role must necessarily be more circumscribed , serving as a tool that supports and extends human teaching rather than substituting for it. The most productive approach, the evidence suggests, is neither uncritical adoption nor blanket rejection, but a discriminating integration informed by robust research, pedagogical wisdom, and a clear-eyed understanding of what technology can and cannot do.", "What emerges most clearly from the accumulated research on educational technology is the importance of context. Technologies that produce positive outcomes in one educational setting frequently fail to do so in another, because the effectiveness of any tool is inseparable from the quality of its implementation, the professional capacity of those who use it, the characteristics of the learners it is intended to serve, and the broader institutional and social environment in which learning takes place. This insight — that there are no technological solutions to fundamentally pedagogical problems — may be the most important lesson that two decades of educational technology research have to teach."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main argument of the passage?", options: ["Digital technology has fundamentally transformed education for the better, and the remaining barriers to its widespread adoption are primarily financial rather than pedagogical or philosophical.", "Educational technology holds genuine promise for personalising learning and extending access, but its effectiveness depends critically on implementation quality, teacher capacity, and contextual fit rather than on the technology itself.", "Research on educational technology has conclusively demonstrated that digital tools are most effective when they replace traditional classroom instruction entirely rather than supplementing the work of human teachers.", "The primary problem with educational technology is that it has been developed and marketed by commercial companies whose financial interests are misaligned with the learning needs of students and teachers."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, for which group of students have adaptive learning platforms shown the most positive outcomes?", options: ["Students from high-income households who have reliable internet access at home and whose parents are able to provide additional academic support alongside the digital platform.", "Advanced students who have already mastered core content and who benefit from algorithmic systems that provide extension activities tailored to their exceptional academic capabilities.", "Students who have fallen behind in core subjects such as mathematics and reading, for whom immediate and individualised feedback helps identify and address specific gaps in understanding.", "University students enrolled in postgraduate programmes who use adaptive platforms to review foundational knowledge before progressing to more advanced and specialised coursework."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does research show about the distribution of benefits from educational technology?", options: ["The benefits of educational technology are distributed equally across all socioeconomic groups when governments ensure that schools in all regions are supplied with adequate devices and internet connectivity.", "MOOC participants from developing countries show significantly better learning outcomes than domestic students at the universities that produce the courses, as they are more highly motivated to complete their studies.", "Students in remote and underserved communities benefit most from educational technology because it gives them access to resources that are otherwise completely unavailable to them.", "Students from higher-income households consistently derive greater benefit from digital learning opportunities than lower-income peers because they are more likely to have reliable access, suitable devices, and adult support."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is described as a critical but frequently underestimated factor in determining whether educational technology produces positive outcomes?", options: ["The speed and reliability of the internet connection available in schools, which determines whether students can access cloud-based learning platforms without disruptive technical interruptions.", "The involvement of parents and guardians in monitoring their children's use of digital learning tools at home, which research has found to be the strongest predictor of academic improvement.", "The age and technical sophistication of the devices provided to students, which affects whether learners can interact with digital tools in the manner intended by their designers.", "Adequate investment in teacher professional development, training, and ongoing support, which enables educators to integrate technology effectively into coherent instructional sequences."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"suboptimal\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["unexpectedly successful given the limited resources invested, suggesting that even minimal technological interventions can produce meaningful improvements in student outcomes", "entirely without merit or educational value, producing outcomes so poor that the programme or initiative must be discontinued and replaced with an alternative approach", "not as good as it could or should be — falling below the level of quality or effectiveness that would be achieved under better conditions or with better preparation", "technically complex and difficult to evaluate accurately, requiring specialised research methodology to distinguish genuine educational gains from improvements attributable to other factors"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"circumscribed\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["limited and restricted within defined boundaries — in this context, technology's role being kept appropriately confined to supporting rather than replacing human teaching", "redefined and expanded to encompass a broader range of educational functions than technology was originally designed or intended to perform in formal learning environments", "enthusiastically embraced and actively promoted by teachers who have received adequate professional development and who understand how to use it effectively in their classrooms", "precisely documented and systematically evaluated through rigorous empirical research that measures its contribution to learning outcomes across diverse educational contexts"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a concern raised about adaptive learning platforms?", options: ["The research evidence for the effectiveness of adaptive learning is more mixed than proponents of these technologies typically acknowledge", "Algorithmic systems may be unable to adequately capture the social, emotional, and motivational dimensions of learning that skilled teachers address", "Many rigorous studies have found effect sizes that are modest at best, suggesting the benefits may be smaller than advocates claim", "The commercial interests of technology companies may lead them to overstate the effectiveness of their platforms in marketing materials distributed to schools"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This concern is particularly salient given evidence suggesting that the habits of attention cultivated by heavy digital media use may persist beyond the screen and interfere with students' engagement with demanding printed texts.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 3 about the relationship between educational technology and social inequality?", options: ["Social inequality has no meaningful effect on educational technology outcomes because the intrinsic motivational appeal of digital learning tools overcomes differences in home environment and parental support.", "Educational technology inevitably reduces social inequality in education because digital resources are cheaper to produce and distribute than traditional textbooks and classroom materials.", "The relationship between educational technology and social inequality is entirely determined by government policy decisions, and well-designed national digital education programmes can eliminate the equity gap completely.", "Without addressing the underlying inequalities in access to devices, internet connectivity, and home support, the deployment of educational technology risks amplifying rather than reducing existing educational achievement gaps."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward the role of educational technology as expressed in paragraph 6?", options: ["Deeply pessimistic, suggesting that the commercial interests driving the educational technology industry make it impossible to achieve the discriminating and evidence-based integration that the research recommends.", "Strongly opposed, concluding that the fundamental relational nature of education makes any significant role for technology inherently incompatible with the goals of genuine intellectual and moral formation.", "Enthusiastically supportive, arguing that well-designed educational technology can fully substitute for human teachers and should be deployed as broadly as possible to maximise learning efficiency.", "Balanced and discriminating, rejecting both uncritical adoption and blanket rejection in favour of context-sensitive integration guided by evidence, pedagogical wisdom, and a realistic understanding of technology's limits."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Urban Habitat: Ecology, Inequity, and the Future of City Living", paragraphs: ["The city, for most of human history, was understood as the antithesis of nature — a space defined by its departure from the biological rhythms, material textures, and ecological relationships that governed life beyond its boundaries. That understanding has been comprehensively revised by several decades of urban ecology research, which has demonstrated that cities are not ecological voids but complex, dynamic ecosystems in their own right, supporting a remarkable diversity of plant, animal, and microbial life that has adapted, often with surprising speed, to the novel conditions of the urban environment. [1] What has not been so thoroughly revised, however, is the political and ethical framework through which cities are planned, governed, and evaluated — a framework that has historically prioritised economic productivity and physical infrastructure over the quality of the living environments inhabited by urban residents, with consequences for human health and wellbeing that are both profound and systematically distributed along lines of race, class, and geography.", "Urban heat islands represent one of the most extensively documented and consequential manifestations of the ecological disruption produced by urban development. The replacement of permeable natural surfaces — soil, vegetation, and water bodies — with impermeable materials such as concrete, asphalt, and building facades creates conditions in which solar radiation is absorbed and retained rather than reflected or dissipated through evapotranspiration. The result is that urban areas are typically several degrees warmer than the surrounding rural and suburban landscape, with densely built inner-city neighbourhoods frequently experiencing the most extreme heat. [2] The health consequences of urban heat — elevated rates of heat stroke, cardiovascular stress, respiratory distress, and heat-related mortality — fall disproportionately on elderly people, young children, outdoor workers, and those living in poorly ventilated housing without access to air conditioning. Critically, these populations are themselves disproportionately concentrated in the urban neighbourhoods that are hottest, least vegetated, and most poorly served by the cooling infrastructure of parks, street trees, and public green spaces.", "The distribution of green space within cities has been the subject of growing research attention, much of it arriving at the same disquieting conclusion: access to parks, gardens, trees, and other forms of urban nature is not a politically neutral amenity distributed according to ecological logic but a resource whose availability is systematically shaped by historical patterns of investment, disinvestment, and racialised land use planning. In numerous cities across the United States, the United Kingdom, and other industrialised nations, formerly redlined or otherwise disinvested neighbourhoods — areas subject to systematic exclusion from mortgage lending, public investment, and quality housing provision — remain, decades after the formal abolition of such policies, significantly hotter, less vegetated, and more polluted than neighbouring areas that benefited from sustained investment. [3] The persistence of these environmental inequities reflects not merely historical injustice but an ongoing failure of urban governance to prioritise the ecological needs of the communities most affected by environmental degradation.", "Air quality represents a second major dimension of environmental inequity in urban settings. Traffic-related air pollution — nitrogen dioxide, particulate matter, and ground-level ozone generated primarily by motor vehicles — is concentrated along major road corridors and in areas with high vehicle density, which in most cities are also areas where lower-income communities and communities of colour are disproportionately represented. Long-term exposure to elevated concentrations of fine particulate matter (PM2.5) is associated with significantly increased risks of respiratory disease, cardiovascular disease, cognitive decline, and premature mortality. Children exposed to elevated levels of traffic-related air pollution show measurable deficits in lung development and, in some studies, in cognitive function and academic achievement — a finding with obvious implications for the reproduction of educational and economic inequality across generations. [4] The spatial coincidence of high pollution exposure and economic disadvantage is not accidental: it reflects decades of planning decisions, zoning regulations, and infrastructure investments that have consistently located noxious land uses and major transport corridors in or adjacent to communities with the least political power to resist them.", "The concept of environmental justice — the principle that all communities, regardless of race, income, or geography, have the right to a clean, safe, and healthy living environment — emerged in the United States in the 1980s as a response to the documented pattern of disproportionate environmental burden borne by communities of colour and low-income communities. Its intellectual and political claims have since been elaborated and globalised, finding expression in urban planning frameworks, international environmental agreements, and the sustainability agendas of cities from Cape Town to Copenhagen. Yet the chasm between the stated commitments of urban governance institutions and the lived environmental realities of disadvantaged urban communities remains, in most cities, distressingly wide. Green infrastructure investments — tree-planting programmes, park expansions, active travel networks — have in some cases produced a phenomenon known as green gentrification, in which environmental improvements raise property values and displace the very communities they were ostensibly designed to benefit.", "The challenge of creating genuinely equitable urban environments is inseparable from broader questions of political power and democratic accountability. Urban planning decisions that shape the distribution of environmental goods and burdens — where parks are located, where highways are routed, where industrial facilities are permitted — are invariably political decisions, and their outcomes reflect the differential capacity of various urban communities to participate in, influence, and hold accountable the institutional processes through which those decisions are made. Communities with greater organisational capacity, financial resources, and political connections consistently secure more favourable environmental outcomes, while marginalised communities — regardless of the formal environmental commitments of the cities in which they live — frequently find that those commitments are honoured more in policy documents than in the physical realities of their neighbourhoods.", "The trajectory of urban environmental equity will be shaped, in the coming decades, by at least three intersecting forces: the intensification of climate change impacts, the growth of the urban population, and the evolving capacity of urban governance institutions to respond to demands for environmental justice. Cities that fail to address their internal environmental inequities will find that the costs of climate adaptation — managing heat, flooding, air quality deterioration, and the health consequences that follow — are borne disproportionately by those least able to bear them. Cities that succeed in building more equitable urban environments will not only reduce the human suffering associated with environmental deprivation but will also build the social cohesion and institutional trust that resilient cities, in a period of accelerating environmental change, will urgently require."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best captures the central argument of the passage?", options: ["Cities are complex ecosystems whose environmental conditions — including heat, green space, and air quality — are distributed inequitably along lines of race and class, and addressing this requires both ecological investment and political reform.", "Air pollution is the single most important environmental challenge facing cities, and resolving it through stricter vehicle emissions standards would effectively eliminate most other forms of urban environmental inequity.", "The environmental justice movement has been entirely successful in compelling urban governments to prioritise the ecological needs of disadvantaged communities over the economic interests of developers and property owners.", "Urban ecology research has demonstrated that cities support rich and diverse ecosystems, and the primary challenge facing urban planners is protecting this biodiversity from further development."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are urban areas typically several degrees warmer than surrounding rural areas?", options: ["The replacement of permeable natural surfaces with impermeable materials such as concrete and asphalt causes solar radiation to be absorbed and retained rather than reflected or dissipated through evapotranspiration.", "The absence of large water bodies in city centres prevents the moderating effect that lakes and rivers have on temperature in rural areas, resulting in more extreme heat during periods of warm weather.", "Urban areas generate significantly more waste heat from industrial processes, vehicle engines, and air conditioning systems than rural areas, which raises ambient temperatures above those of the surrounding landscape.", "Urban populations consume more energy per person than rural populations, and the heat released as a by-product of this energy consumption accumulates in densely built areas with limited air circulation."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does research into the distribution of urban green space consistently find?", options: ["Access to parks and urban nature is distributed equitably in most modern cities, as planning authorities have successfully corrected the imbalances created by historical patterns of racialised land use.", "Urban green space is primarily concentrated in suburban areas because land values in inner-city neighbourhoods are too high to make park development economically viable for municipal authorities.", "The availability of urban green space is determined primarily by geographic and climatic factors rather than by political and economic decisions, making it broadly consistent across different urban neighbourhoods.", "Formerly disinvested and redlined neighbourhoods remain significantly hotter, less vegetated, and more polluted than areas that benefited from sustained investment, reflecting both historical injustice and ongoing governance failures."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what effect does long-term exposure to elevated PM2.5 concentrations have on children?", options: ["It is associated with measurable deficits in lung development and, in some studies, in cognitive function and academic achievement, with implications for the reproduction of inequality across generations.", "It increases children's susceptibility to infectious diseases by suppressing immune function, leading to higher rates of school absence and lower academic attainment in affected communities.", "It causes immediate and severe respiratory symptoms that require hospitalisation, placing a significant burden on urban health systems in neighbourhoods near major road corridors.", "It primarily affects children's social development, reducing their capacity for cooperation and communication in ways that persist into adulthood and limit their professional opportunities."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"chasm\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a technical term used in environmental science to describe the measurable difference in air quality, temperature, and green space availability between adjacent urban neighbourhoods", "a very large and significant gap or divide — in this context, between the formal environmental commitments of urban institutions and the environmental realities experienced by disadvantaged communities", "a legal dispute between community organisations and municipal governments over the failure to implement environmental justice policies within the timelines specified in official planning documents", "a careful and systematic evaluation process used by urban governance institutions to measure the distance between their stated environmental commitments and the outcomes they have achieved"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"noxious\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["harmful or poisonous to health — in this context referring to land uses such as industrial facilities and major road corridors that generate pollution damaging to human health", "politically controversial and generating significant public opposition from local residents, making it difficult for authorities to obtain planning approval in any neighbourhood", "economically unproductive and therefore considered undesirable by property developers and municipal planners seeking to maximise the commercial value of urban land", "visually unattractive and detrimental to the aesthetic quality of the urban environment, reducing property values and discouraging investment in affected neighbourhoods"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Urban coyotes, peregrine falcons nesting on skyscraper ledges, and heat-tolerant plant species colonising pavement cracks are among the more visible examples of this adaptive capacity.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 5 about the phenomenon of green gentrification?", options: ["Green gentrification is primarily a problem in North American cities and does not occur in European or Asian urban contexts, where stronger tenancy protections prevent the displacement of low-income residents by rising property values.", "The solution to green gentrification is to halt all green infrastructure investments in disadvantaged urban neighbourhoods until governments have developed policies that guarantee no displacement of existing residents will occur.", "Green gentrification demonstrates that environmental improvements always benefit all urban residents equally, regardless of their income level or tenure status, confirming that green infrastructure is a reliable tool for reducing inequality.", "Environmental improvements in disadvantaged urban neighbourhoods can inadvertently harm the communities they are designed to help by raising property values and displacing low-income residents who can no longer afford to remain."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 6 about why marginalised urban communities consistently receive less favourable environmental outcomes?", options: ["Urban planning decisions reflect differences in political power and organisational capacity, meaning that communities with less influence over institutional processes are systematically exposed to worse environmental conditions.", "Environmental inequity in cities is entirely the result of deliberate discrimination by planning authorities, and it would be resolved quickly if individual planners with racist attitudes were removed from decision-making roles.", "The unfavourable environmental conditions in marginalised communities are primarily the result of their geographic location in areas with naturally poorer air quality and less suitable land for park development.", "Marginalised communities typically prefer denser housing and industrial proximity because these conditions offer lower rents and easier access to employment, making their environmental situation a matter of voluntary choice."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward the prospects for urban environmental equity as expressed in the final paragraph?", options: ["Completely pessimistic, concluding that the structural inequalities embedded in urban governance systems are so entrenched that meaningful environmental equity is effectively impossible to achieve within existing political frameworks.", "Unconditionally optimistic, arguing that the growing global urban sustainability movement has already resolved most environmental equity challenges and that the remaining problems are minor and easily addressed.", "Critically engaged and conditionally hopeful, warning that cities failing to address internal inequities will impose disproportionate climate costs on the most vulnerable, while suggesting that equitable cities can build the resilience and trust they will need.", "Primarily concerned with macroeconomic efficiency, arguing that environmental equity investments are justified mainly by the economic costs of treating the health consequences of pollution and heat rather than by considerations of justice or rights."], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
];
